      "dom_id": "question-39768",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve $\\sqrt{6x+1}=3x-1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To solve the equation $\\sqrt{6x+1}=3x-1,$ we raise both sides to the power of $2.$  This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6x+1} & =3x-1 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{6x+1})}^{2} & =(3x-1)^{2} \\\\ 6x+1 & =9{x}^{2}-6x+1 \\\\ 9{x}^{2}-12x & =0 \\\\ 3x(3x-4) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\n- First, we check whether the obtained value $x=0$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6(0)+1} & \\overset{?}{=}3(0)-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=}-1 \\\\ 1 & \\ne -1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=0$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we discard it.\n\n- Second, we check whether the obtained value $x=\\frac{4}{3}$ is a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6(\\frac{4}{3})+1} & \\overset{?}{=}3(\\frac{4}{3})-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{8+1} & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIndeed, the value $x=\\frac{4}{3}$ does satisfy the original equation.\n\nTherefore, the only solution of $\\sqrt{6x+1}=3x-1$  is $x=\\frac{4}{3}.$",
